That JSON confirms the cause. Your screenshot shows the bug happening: only failure has data in that hour, so it gets the first palette colour (blue).

## 1. Root cause (two settings combine)

```json
"style": { "palette": "datadog16", "order_by": "values", ... }   // on the REQUEST, not per formula
```

- **The palette is set on the request.** Datadog colours each series by its **position**, not by its name.
- **`order_by: "values"`** sorts series by their values, so the positions change whenever the numbers change.
- **Result:** in a 5-min window where success has no data, failure becomes series 1 and turns blue. On longer windows the order (and so the colour) changes again, which gives red. The colour is set by the data, never pinned to "failure".

## 2. Fix: give each formula a fixed colour

```json
{
  "title": "EDI Device Capability Workflow Success vs Failure",
  "type": "timeseries",
  "requests": [
    {
      "formulas": [
        {
          "alias": "Device Capability Workflow Success",
          "formula": "success",
          "style": { "palette": "datadog16", "palette_index": <SUCCESS_INDEX> }
        },
        {
          "alias": "Device Capability Workflow Failure",
          "formula": "failure",
          "style": { "palette": "datadog16", "palette_index": <PINK_INDEX> }
        }
      ],
      "queries": [ ...unchanged... ],
      "response_format": "timeseries",
      "style": { "palette": "datadog16", "order_by": "tags", "line_type": "solid", "line_width": "normal" },
      "display_type": "line"
    }
  ]
}
```

Don't guess the index numbers. In the **Edit** tab, click the colour swatch next to each formula and pick pink for failure and the success colour from the spec. Then switch to the **JSON** tab, and Datadog will have written the exact `style` block for you. Copy that.

